Dapper will not automatically populate the nested `AccessPolicyRules` list through your generic DAL method. Query the flat DTO rows first, group them by policy, and then construct the models.

First, ensure the SQL aliases match the DTO property names exactly:

```sql
ALTER PROCEDURE dbo.usp_GetAccessPolicy
AS
BEGIN
    SET NOCOUNT ON;

    SELECT
        AP.Id AS AccessPolicyId,
        AP.PolicyCode AS AccessPolicyCode,
        AP.PolicyName AS AccessPolicyName,
        AP.IsEnabled AS IsAccessPolicyEnabled,
        AP.CreatedDate AS AccessPolicyCreatedDate,
        AP.CreatedBy AS AccessPolicyCreatedBy,
        AP.LastModifiedDate AS AccessPolicyLastModifiedDate,
        AP.LastModifiedBy AS AccessPolicyLastModifiedBy,

        APR.Id AS AccessPolicyRuleId,
        APR.AccessPolicyId AS AccessPolicyRuleAccessPolicyId,
        APR.RuleName AS AccessPolicyRuleName,
        APR.WindowSeconds AS AccessPolicyRuleWindowSeconds,
        APR.MaximumAccessCount AS AccessPolicyRuleMaximumAccessCount,
        APR.IsEnabled AS IsAccessPolicyRuleEnabled,
        APR.CreatedDate AS AccessPolicyRuleCreatedDate,
        APR.CreatedBy AS AccessPolicyRuleCreatedBy,
        APR.LastModifiedDate AS AccessPolicyRuleLastModifiedDate,
        APR.LastModifiedBy AS AccessPolicyRuleLastModifiedBy
    FROM dbo.t_AccessPolicy AS AP
    INNER JOIN dbo.t_AccessPolicyRule AS APR
        ON APR.AccessPolicyId = AP.Id
    ORDER BY
        AP.Id,
        APR.Id;
END;
```

Then change the C# method to query `AccessPolicyDbRowDto`:

```csharp
public async Task<List<AccessPolicy>> GetAccessPolicy(
    GetAccessPolicyRequest request)
{
    DapperDal dal = new DapperDal(_globalSettings.ConnString);

    IEnumerable<AccessPolicyDbRowDto> dbRows =
        await dal.ExecuteQueryAsync<AccessPolicyDbRowDto>(
            "usp_GetAccessPolicy",
            null,
            CommandType.StoredProcedure,
            DapperDal.CommandDirection.Select);

    List<AccessPolicy> accessPolicies = dbRows
        .GroupBy(row => row.AccessPolicyId)
        .Select(policyGroup =>
        {
            AccessPolicyDbRowDto firstRow = policyGroup.First();

            return new AccessPolicy
            {
                Id = firstRow.AccessPolicyId,
                PolicyCode = firstRow.AccessPolicyCode,
                PolicyName = firstRow.AccessPolicyName,
                IsEnabled = firstRow.IsAccessPolicyEnabled,
                CreatedDate = firstRow.AccessPolicyCreatedDate,
                CreatedBy = firstRow.AccessPolicyCreatedBy,
                LastModifiedDate =
                    firstRow.AccessPolicyLastModifiedDate,
                LastModifiedBy =
                    firstRow.AccessPolicyLastModifiedBy,

                AccessPolicyRules = policyGroup
                    .Select(row => new AccessPolicyRule
                    {
                        Id = row.AccessPolicyRuleId,
                        AccessPolicyId =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleAccessPolicyId,
                        RuleName = row.AccessPolicyRuleName,
                        WindowSeconds =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleWindowSeconds,
                        MaximumAccessCount =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleMaximumAccessCount,
                        IsEnabled =
                            row.IsAccessPolicyRuleEnabled,
                        CreatedDate =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleCreatedDate,
                        CreatedBy =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleCreatedBy,
                        LastModifiedDate =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleLastModifiedDate,
                        LastModifiedBy =
                            row.AccessPolicyRuleLastModifiedBy
                    })
                    .OrderBy(rule => rule.Id)
                    .ToList()
            };
        })
        .OrderBy(policy => policy.Id)
        .ToList();

    return accessPolicies;
}
```

The database returns flat rows such as:

```text
Policy 1 + Rule 1
Policy 1 + Rule 2
Policy 1 + Rule 3
Policy 2 + Rule 4
```

The `GroupBy(row => row.AccessPolicyId)` converts these into:

```text
Policy 1
  Rules: 1, 2, 3

Policy 2
  Rules: 4
```

Your original query should not use:

```csharp
ExecuteQueryAsync<AccessPolicy>
```

because the returned SQL columns represent both a policy and a rule. It must use:

```csharp
ExecuteQueryAsync<AccessPolicyDbRowDto>
```

Also, `request` is currently unused. You can remove it unless you plan to add policy filters. If policies without any rules must also be returned, use a `LEFT JOIN` and make all rule fields in `AccessPolicyDbRowDto` nullable.
